                                            <td class="watches">0</td>
                                            <td class="thumbnail"></td>
                                            <td class="timeoriginalestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timeestimate"></td>
                                            <td class="timespent"></td>
                                            <td class="workratio">&nbsp;
</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      I&#39;ve a NullPointerException when I set a SOAP header by using an XmlObject:<br/>
<br/>
final XmlObject userDetails = getDetails();<br/>
final QName name = EndUserDetailsDocumentXML.type.getDocumentElementName();<br/>
final Header userDetailsHeader = new Header(name, userDetails, new XmlBeansDataBinding());                <br/>
userDetailsHeader.setDirection(Header.Direction.DIRECTION_OUT);<br/>
message.getHeaders().add(userDetailsHeader);<br/>
<br/>
<br/>
<br/>
The problem is that the XmlBeans implementation is guessing that the type class of the message part is not null (part.getTypeClass()). But it&#39;s always null, see the class org.apache.cxf.binding.soap.interceptor.SoapOutInterceptor:<br/>
<br/>
                    if (b != null) {<br/>
                        MessagePartInfo part = new MessagePartInfo(header.getName(), null);<br/>
                        part.setConcreteName(header.getName());<br/>
                        b.createWriter(XMLStreamWriter.class)<br/>
                            .write(header.getObject(), part, writer);<br/>
<br/>
<br/>
<br/>
Later in org.apache.cxf.xmlbeans.DataWriterImpl (line 71):<br/>
<br/>
    public void write(Object obj, MessagePartInfo part, XMLStreamWriter output) {<br/>
        try {<br/>
            Class&lt;?&gt; typeClass = part.getTypeClass();<br/>
            if (!XmlObject.class.isAssignableFrom(typeClass)) {
  </td>
