    </td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      Consider the SXC Reference on the CXF JAXB page ( http://cxf.apache.org/docs/jaxb.html ):<br/>
<br/>
Speedy alternative to the JAXB Reference Implementation<br/>
If you are looking for a performance boost inside your application, you may want to examine SXC, which accelerates the JAXB runtime.<br/>
<br/>
The link to SXC is to here: http://sxc.codehaus.org/.   When going to this URL, you see this, &quot;There is currently no description associated with this project.&quot;  Exploring further, there are no developers on the SXC team, based on documentation (http://sxc.codehaus.org/team-list.html).  Also, the last download is from 2007 (http://sxc.codehaus.org/Download).  Most important though, it did not pass the TCK (http://sxc.codehaus.org/JAXB).<br/>
<br/>
As such, it looks like this project is no longer supported.  Is it safe to reference an abandoned project?  I would advice removing the link or encouraging the SXC folks to reopen the project for at least, basic maintenance purposes.<br/>
<br/>
If you refer someone to use SXC, they integrate it, find a problem using it, then can&#39;t get it fixed... that&#39;s not cool.<br/>
<br/>
&gt;&gt;&gt; Or... keep the reference, but put in a note on the state of the project...  querying the SXC project owner (if they are reachable) would be the best path to get a clear picture of where the project is.<br/>
<br/>
Thanks.
  </td>
